    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d155">

                <pb facs="0333" n="237" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg237"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">441.11 W 892/53: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Houghton</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-06-16T18:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 16, 1926—6
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 16—4:30
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>131. This morning Chamberlain sent for me and after some discussion stated that
                    he had decided to withdraw his objection to Phenix’s visit and had just cabled
                    Howard that Phenix might come in September if the naval delegation could
                    accompany him. Chamberlain added that he would not object if Phenix came without
                    the naval delegation or if he preferred coming in August, although he believed
                    September preferable. As you know August is the worst possible month for
                    Phenix’s visit, owing to vacations. Chamberlain incidentally remarked that
                    before he went to Geneva he instructed Tyrrell<anchor n="20" xml:id="d155-f1"/>
                    to withdraw all objection to Phenix’s visit if, after Howard’s interview and
                    receipt of my telegram, you still thought it desirable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Houghton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d155-f1" n="20" xml:id="d155-f1-content">
                <p>Sir William G. Tyrrell, British Permanent Under Secretary of State for Foreign
